WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.030
Film Courage: …On this same event [referenced
in another video] that had this panel of crime

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.250
writers, they were fascinating and talking
about different things and when asked they

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:12.770
said the thing feared most because they write
about crime and some of their descriptions

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.639
are gruesome, what do they fear most?

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.860
The biggest fear was that the well is dry
for ideas.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.350
I thought this was interesting.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.730
Orson Oblowitz, Filmmaker/Photographer: The
well is dry for ideas.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.550
Film Courage: Does this ever scare you?

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.859
Orson: Yes, all the time.

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:31.179
I think that we all deal with trying to tell
a unique story.

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:35.930
It’s so funny, it actually goes back to…and
I keep using people’s quotes but I was reading

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.860
in an interview that Francis Ford Coppola
did last week and he’s 80 years old.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:47.870
He’s trying to make a big movie and they
asked him what makes an artist as a filmmaker?

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:52.440
Do you have any advice to young entrepreneurial
filmmakers or artists.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.500
He goes well if you want to be an entrepreneurial
that’s one thing.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:04.230
But he goes if you want to be an artist as
a filmmaker just start off with the one thesis

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:12.530
which is this: there is a one in a trillion
chance that this zygote was created that created

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:17.320
this single individual (yourself or any of
the other 10 billion people on this earth).

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.220
Understand that everyone has an extremely
individual story no matter who they are and

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.520
you can be an artist.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:30.280
And I think that goes back to the well will
never be dry because every story is individual.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.140
Every story is specific.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:43.600
And I think as a filmmaker you just have to
go out and fight to find that story, it’s

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.300
not always going to come to you.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.020
But there’s always a story, there’s always
an experience, there’s always a history

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:55.030
that someone or something has and it’s your
responsibility as a storyteller to go find

00:01:55.030 --> 00:02:04.390
it and manicure it and help it grow as if
it was a flower or garden until it finally

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.780
comes to fruition.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.280
Film Courage: Which would you say you want
to be?

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.319
An artist or an entrepreneurial filmmaker?

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:18.440
Because it seems like the days of the artists,
you almost can’t just be that?

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:24.090
Orson: I mean I’d love to just be an artist
walking around with a camera and thinking

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.280
but we all have to pay rent.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.610
But in the end I don’t know I never thought
of myself trying to be like an entrepreneur

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.370
or trying to reinvent the wheel.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:39.490
I just want to tell stories and I hope that
those stories resonate with some people and

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:46.970
if along the way you happen to be successful
in business, that’s amazing.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:47.970
We all want it.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.780
We all want a more comfortable life than we
have at the moment.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.950
I think even the richest people are always
looking to make their life more comfortable,

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:56.950
make their life better.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.050
Whatever those reasons are.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:06.250
But I think Queen [referring to Orson’s
film THE QUEEN OF HOLLYWOOD BLVD.] and the

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:12.790
other two films, I think I have a predilection
I like focusing on people who don’t necessarily

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:18.470
have their stories told not as if I’m not
going to go to the homeless guy on the street

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.270
and be like “I’m going to tell your story.”

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:28.720
Maybe one day but I’m not saying it has
to be extremes all the time.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:35.129
But I like telling stories about people in
their small worlds and showing that everyone’s

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:43.420
world is really important to them and everyone
has their own individual evolution so I don’t

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.209
know I guess I want to be an artist and I
guess I’d love to be an artist (consider

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:54.880
myself an artist) but I think it’s a business
and we’ve all got to do business as well.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.550
Film Courage: How do you start a screenplay.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:03.599
I mean since you love character that aren’t
mainstream and I am the same way.

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:09.830
I don’t resonate with a lot of “chick
flicks” and it’s not that…to me I can’t

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:10.830
identify with it.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.150
It’s too perfect for me.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.819
I find myself getting squeamish.

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:18.169
I like something with more edges around it.

00:04:18.169 --> 00:04:23.699
Orson: Yes, I like broken people because I
feel like one as well.

00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:26.020
I like people who are conflicted.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.699
It’s so weird…how do I start a screenplay?

00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:29.699
All different ways.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:32.099
I can’t tell you.

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:36.960
The biggest part of the process it’s the
dumbest thing in the world - the empty page

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:43.379
is the scariest thing in the world so it’s
just like the way I start writing is say I’m

00:04:43.379 --> 00:04:50.479
a photographer as well so most of my films
that I’ve written are based off of an image

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:51.650
maybe that I’ve taken.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:55.379
That’s kind of where I start a lot is someone
who I have captured through an image either

00:04:55.379 --> 00:04:58.110
the image really ends up having nothing to
do with the story.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:02.970
It’s kind of like there’s an image burned
into my head and I start with that and I allow

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.629
that to take its evolution.

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:11.610
I’m a pretty sloppy writer in that I just
start writing and I let the story and the

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.650
characters guide me because I mainly write
films that are seen by a singular POV of the

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:17.879
main character.

00:05:17.879 --> 00:05:22.460
So because of that I kind of follow that character
and I learn about them and as I learn about

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.800
them a plot evolves.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:31.210
But that’s kind of how I start on a film
I’m writing it from the beginning is it

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:36.830
mostly comes from an image and then that image
mostly gives me an idea for a character and

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.279
then I follow that character and then that
world starts to build and then I rewrite a

00:05:40.279 --> 00:05:43.680
lot.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:51.240
Sometimes it will come, someone goes “Hey
I might have some money to make a movie.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.860
I have a small idea, what do you think?”

00:05:52.860 --> 00:06:00.430
And that idea because a giant kernel or it
becomes a kernel that pops and suddenly you

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.830
run with that idea because there’s a real
possibility of making it and you make it your

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:05.830
own.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.520
I mean that’s a big thing I’ve learned
about filmmaking is you’ve got to be able

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.960
to sometimes make someone else’s idea your
own and that’s a really interesting process

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.490
as well in its own right.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:22.309
But for writing with me its the image comes
first and then the character and then I follow

00:06:22.309 --> 00:06:28.399
that character and I find a world and I find
a plot within that character’s world and

00:06:28.399 --> 00:06:29.399
I go on the ride.

00:06:29.399 --> 00:06:32.610
I try to be the first audience member and
enjoy that process.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:40.110
I like to write fast and this could also be
to my own detriment but I don’t spend a

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.990
lot of time on a screenplay.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:47.680
I try to just write fast, get it out and push
it out there and then start going through

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:48.680
the rewriting process.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:57.210
I show it to a group of people that I trust
or attach some how to the project but mainly

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:02.399
you always need a good sounding board and
it’s one of the most important elements

00:07:02.399 --> 00:07:07.039
of the writing process (of every process of
the film) is having people that you can go

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:10.610
“What do you think because I’m lost?”

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:18.910
And finding that nature of people that can
give you notes and ideas and tell you when

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:21.860
you’re right or wrong or too stuck.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.129
Film Courage: With [The Queen of Hollywood
Blvd.] was there an image?

00:07:24.129 --> 00:07:28.039
I know you said you were in a conflicted place
when you wrote it but was there an image [that

00:07:28.039 --> 00:07:29.039
prompted it]?

00:07:29.039 --> 00:07:34.780
Orson: Yes, there was a photo of a woman on
Western Avenue wearing a leopard skin coat

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:43.080
that I took out of the side of a car and the
film started with an idea of a woman in a

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.080
leopard skin coat.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.449
Film Courage: Was she waiting for a bus?

00:07:45.449 --> 00:07:46.449
What was she doing?

00:07:46.449 --> 00:07:49.659
Orson: I’m not sure what she was doing,
it was probably not good.

00:07:49.659 --> 00:07:52.629
Film Courage: Okay…she wasn’t waiting
for an Uber.

00:07:52.629 --> 00:07:54.860
Orson: She had seen better days.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:56.800
But it was cool I know the image.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:01.119
I can see it in my head right now and it’s
actually funny enough now next to where I

00:08:01.119 --> 00:08:05.389
took the image was in front of a church which
is now next door to my office where I write

00:08:05.389 --> 00:08:06.610
and edit and stuff.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:07.990
So years later…

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:09.610
Film Courage: Oh wow, how ironic?

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:10.729
That is really interesting.

00:08:10.729 --> 00:08:15.020
Orson: Yes it is, I was thinking about it.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:22.400
But I remember that image and from that image…that
image just spurned this character and then

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:30.740
the character I pulled from my life and tons
of real-life situations and scenarios and

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:33.750
people and then other photos I had taken.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:40.550
I wrote about those characters and then some
of them changed on set…everything.

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:42.250
The whole writing process is such an evolution.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:46.790
But yes, it really did come from a photo out
of the side of a car.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:49.061
Film Courage: On Western Avenue in Hollywood?

00:08:49.061 --> 00:08:49.080
Orson: On Western Avenue, yes.

